# Cursor IDE Environment Briefing

## Canonical Source

The body below mirrors `cursor-environment.md` in the **Cortex data layer**
at `/mnt/torus/mcp-data/files/agent-identity/` (sandbox path:
`agent-identity/cursor-environment.md`).

∀ edits to the briefing text below: first update
`agent-identity/cursor-environment.md` via
`fs(sandbox="cortex", op="write", ...)`, then mirror the change here.
Do not edit this rule in isolation.

## Scope

This file orients any agent that boots inside the Cursor IDE on this
workspace. It is environmental, not identity-shaped: it describes the
worksite, the affordances, and the conventions of the place — not who you
are or how you should sound.

## Worksite

When a session starts here, it is not starting from zero. The repository
holds accumulated work — commits, decisions, migrations, running services —
and work in this environment happens inside that body directly, not from a
distance.

Access in the IDE is different from access on other surfaces. Source can be
inspected, call paths traced, diffs read, commands run. Writes persist.
Services restart or fail based on what is done here. This is implementation
territory: the place where architecture meets syntax, where intention meets
the stubborn detail of the actual repository.

## Workspace rules

The `.cursor/rules/` directory contains the operating rules for this
environment: service lifecycle, transport conventions, API surface
invariants, quality gates, tool usage. Some are always-applied; others load
on demand based on globs or task triggers. Read them, follow them, surface
conflicts rather than silently violating them.

Workspace-rule catalog and discovery conventions live in `.cursor/rules/index.mdc`.

## IDE affordances

- **Cursor commands** — `.cursor/commands/*.md` define repeatable workflows
  (`/cortex-brief`, `/implement-plan`, `/session-end`, `/agent-bus`,
  `/todo`). Invoke by name; the command body is loaded into the prompt.
- **Sticky slash commands (`<cursor_commands>`)** — when a slash command
  stays attached to the composer thread, Cursor injects it into
  `<cursor_commands>` on **every** turn (before `<user_query>`). This is IDE
  behavior, not hooks or repo config. Appending freeform text after a slash
  command in the same input (e.g. `/session-end` + "share a handoff…") causes
  the compound block to replay for the rest of the thread. **Agent rule:**
  `<user_query>` is operator intent; `<cursor_commands>` alone is harness noise
  — ignore silently unless `<user_query>` also requests that command. **Operator
  fix:** clear the pinned command chip or start a fresh chat.
- **Hooks** — `.cursor/hooks.json` (when present) wires shell scripts to
  agent events.
- **Agent transcripts** — past chat transcripts live under
  `~/.cursor/projects/<workspace-mount>/agent-transcripts/<uuid>/<uuid>.jsonl`.
  Cite parent transcripts by uuid; never cite subagent transcripts.
  **Do not Read raw JSONL for bind/harvest** — it embeds full tool payloads.
  Use `cortex(assemble_transcript, jsonl_path=<uuid>/<uuid>.jsonl, session_id=…)`
  for the server-assembled verbatim layer (tool JSON collapsed to
  `[tool call: NAME]` markers; ~50%+ smaller). Search assembled markdown for
  keywords when a full arc read is unnecessary. Web session_close: Use the
  `web-transcript-preprocessing` skill before close.
- **Terminals folder** — open terminals are mirrored as text files at
  `~/.cursor/projects/<workspace-mount>/terminals/<id>.txt`. Each file's
  first ~10 lines carry metadata (pid, cwd, last command, exit code); the
  rest is rolling output. Read these files directly; do not announce them
  to the user.
- **Manage TUI via tmux (operator host)** — `./manage` commonly runs in
  **tmux session 0, window 0** (`tmux` target `0:0`). Cursor seats MAY
  operate that TUI directly: `tmux capture-pane -t 0:0 -p` to read the
  screen; `tmux send-keys -t 0:0 …` for keystrokes (bindings like `q`
  Quit, `s` Services). Confirm the pane shows manage (footer `q Quit` /
  topology) before sending keys — do not assume every `0:0` is manage.
  Prefer MCP `manage(...)` for lifecycle when the socket is up; use tmux
  when the TUI itself is the subject (quit path, Sync+Restart, screen
  navigation). Guard: ¬ quit while a charter tick / fleet deploy is busy
  unless the operator asks.
- **Recently viewed / open files** — surfaced in the context envelope as
  hints; treat as suggestions, not authoritative scope.

## Working directory and sandbox routing

- **Workspace path** — the workspace root for this session is the
  repository checkout (e.g. `/mnt/torus/projects/<repo>`). Shell commands
  start there unless `working_directory` is overridden.
- **MCP `fs` sandboxes** — file I/O routes through one of two sandboxes:
  - `sandbox="workspaces"` for repo source, config, tasks, docs. Paths
    MUST include the repo-name prefix
    (e.g. `cortex://notes/system/specs/foo.md`).
  - `sandbox="cortex"` for Cortex-managed documents under `/data/files/`
    (notes, agent-identity, agent-skills, uploads).
- **Response-store flagged payloads** — large MCP responses may return a
  flag rather than inline content; resolve via the indicated retrieval
  call. See `mcp-tool-awareness_ws.mdc` for the full convention.

## Tool surface

The IDE exposes specialized tools for file I/O, search, and editing
(`Read`, `Glob`, `Grep`, `StrReplace`, `Write`, `EditNotebook`, `Delete`)
alongside `Shell` for terminal operations. Prefer the specialized tools
over shell equivalents (`cat`, `find`, `sed`, `awk`); the specialized
tools are bounded, indexed, and faster for the IDE.

MCP tools are reached via `CallMcpTool` against the configured servers
(see `mcp-tool-awareness_ws.mdc` for the calling convention —
`vortex-code` / `vortex-life`; legacy `user-vortex` retired).

## Shell environment

- Python venv: `$HOME/.venvs/universal` (Python 3.12).
- `PYTHONPATH`: `libs/` is added via `sitecustomize.py`; libraries import
  directly (e.g. `from process_ipc.core import ...`).
- Logging: ecosystem components use `universal_logging.get_logger(...)`,
  not stdlib `logging` directly (see `core_ws.mdc`).
- `tmp/`: ephemeral; never committed; never referenced from durable
  knowledge (rules, cortex notes, journal entries).

## Operational topology

- Stargate, RAG, Cortex API, agent-bus, and git-integration-worker
  are host-managed via `./manage` (subprocess or uvicorn on loopback TCP). Gateway
  runs in edge containers; MCP runs in its own container. Lifecycle: `manage` MCP
  tool against the local control socket — not systemd by default.
- **claude.ai / Cowork is resilient to all service restarts** (mcp, **cdp_ask**,
  and the rest). `identity(CSE)=chat_url`. Restart drops attach handles (socket,
  Playwright, `execution_id`); the Chrome tab survives and recovers after
  `wait_healthy` (followup/reattach or continuity hop). Do **not** skip `mcp` or
  `cdp_ask` `sync_restart` to “protect Cowork attach.” Rule:
  `restart-drain-discipline`.
- **Host UDS paths** (code SOT: `libs/transport_utils/client_factory.py`; snapshot:
  `~/.gateway/topology.yaml`):

  | Socket | Default path | Env override |
  |---|---|---|
  | Manage controller | `/tmp/universal-protocol/manage.sock` | `MANAGE_SOCKET` |
  | Cortex API | `/tmp/universal-protocol/cortex-api.sock` | `CORTEX_API_SOCK` |
  | Agent bus | `/tmp/universal-protocol/agent-bus.sock` | `AGENT_BUS_SOCK` |
  | RAG | `/tmp/universal-protocol/rag.sock` | `RAG_SOCKET_PATH` |

  Agents: prefer MCP `manage(action="status")` over shell `test -S`. Do **not**
  probe `…/universal-llm-gateway/manage.sock` or `~/.gateway/manage.sock`.
- Logs land under `/tmp/logs/<service>/` as a fallback; the Event Service
  (`scripts/query-events`, or the `observability` MCP tool) is the primary
  query surface.
- Topology details (nodes, ports, federation) are in `topology_ws.mdc`.
